{"black_box_warning":{"current_value":"No FDA boxed warning identified in selected current DailyMed label.","fda_source_urls":["https://www.accessdata.fda.gov/drugsatfda_docs/label/2024/021129s056%2C020882s057%2C020235s076lbl.pdf"],"proposed_value":"No FDA boxed warning identified in current FDA-approved Neurontin (gabapentin) prescribing information.","rationale":"The no-boxed-warning conclusion is supported by the FDA-approved Neurontin label, but the current row uses DailyMed and a Horizant (gabapentin enacarbil) label. DailyMed is not permissible for this field, and Horizant is not the same product as gabapentin.","requires_user_approval":false,"status":"incorrect"},"blocking_questions":[],"fact_checks":[{"current_value":"gabapentin","field":"generic_name","proposed_value":"gabapentin","rationale":"FDA labeling identifies Neurontin as gabapentin.","requires_user_approval":false,"status":"verified","supporting_sources":["https://www.accessdata.fda.gov/drugsatfda_docs/label/2024/021129s056%2C020882s057%2C020235s076lbl.pdf"]},{"current_value":"","field":"alternate_generic_names","proposed_value":"","rationale":"No separate alternate generic name was identified for gabapentin. GBP is an abbreviation used in trial abstracts, not an alternate generic name.","requires_user_approval":false,"status":"verified","supporting_sources":["https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/10448830/"]},{"current_value":"","field":"pubmed_search_aliases","proposed_value":"GBP","rationale":"Several PubMed abstracts use GBP as an abbreviation for gabapentin; this is useful as a search alias rather than an alternate generic name.","requires_user_approval":false,"status":"missing","supporting_sources":["https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/10448830/","https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/1907907/"]},{"current_value":"Gralise; Horizant; Neurontin","field":"trade_names","proposed_value":"Gralise; Neurontin","rationale":"Neurontin and Gralise are gabapentin products. Horizant is gabapentin enacarbil, a prodrug, and the FDA label states it is not the same medicine as gabapentin products such as Neurontin or Gralise and should not be used in their place.","requires_user_approval":true,"status":"incorrect","supporting_sources":["https://www.accessdata.fda.gov/drugsatfda_docs/label/2024/021129s056%2C020882s057%2C020235s076lbl.pdf","https://www.accessdata.fda.gov/drugsatfda_docs/label/2023/022544s029lbl.pdf","https://www.accessdata.fda.gov/drugsatfda_docs/label/2020/022399s010lbl.pdf"]},{"current_value":"Yes","field":"available_in_us","proposed_value":"Yes","rationale":"Gabapentin/Neurontin is FDA-approved and has current FDA labeling.","requires_user_approval":false,"status":"verified","supporting_sources":["https://www.accessdata.fda.gov/drugsatfda_docs/label/2024/021129s056%2C020882s057%2C020235s076lbl.pdf"]},{"current_value":"Focal","field":"epilepsy_type","proposed_value":"Focal","rationale":"FDA labeling indicates adjunctive therapy for partial-onset seizures, which corresponds to focal seizures.","requires_user_approval":false,"status":"verified","supporting_sources":["https://www.accessdata.fda.gov/drugsatfda_docs/label/2024/021129s056%2C020882s057%2C020235s076lbl.pdf"]},{"current_value":"Capsule; Liquid; Tablet","field":"filter_formulation","proposed_value":"Capsule; Liquid; Tablet","rationale":"FDA labeling lists capsules, tablets, and oral solution.","requires_user_approval":false,"status":"verified","supporting_sources":["https://www.accessdata.fda.gov/drugsatfda_docs/label/2024/021129s056%2C020882s057%2C020235s076lbl.pdf"]},{"current_value":"Capsule; tablet; oral solution","field":"formulations_available","proposed_value":"Capsule; tablet; oral solution","rationale":"FDA labeling lists capsules, tablets, and oral solution.","requires_user_approval":false,"status":"verified","supporting_sources":["https://www.accessdata.fda.gov/drugsatfda_docs/label/2024/021129s056%2C020882s057%2C020235s076lbl.pdf"]},{"current_value":"Not metabolized; not an enzyme inducer/inhibitor","field":"enzyme_inducing_or_inhibiting","proposed_value":"Not appreciably metabolized; no clinically meaningful hepatic enzyme induction/inhibition identified","rationale":"FDA labeling states gabapentin is not appreciably metabolized and does not interfere with metabolism of commonly coadministered antiepileptic drugs; NCBI review sources also describe minimal metabolism and lack of liver-enzyme induction.","requires_user_approval":false,"status":"verified","supporting_sources":["https://www.accessdata.fda.gov/drugsatfda_docs/label/2024/021129s056%2C020882s057%2C020235s076lbl.pdf","https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/7693432/","https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK548252/"]},{"current_value":"Inducer; No major enzyme effect","field":"filter_enzyme_effect","proposed_value":"No major enzyme effect","rationale":"The 'Inducer' value contradicts the row's own enzyme statement and FDA/NCBI sources. Gabapentin is not a hepatic enzyme inducer.","requires_user_approval":true,"status":"incorrect","supporting_sources":["https://www.accessdata.fda.gov/drugsatfda_docs/label/2024/021129s056%2C020882s057%2C020235s076lbl.pdf","https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/7693432/"]},{"current_value":"Not metabolized; renal excretion","field":"major_organ_for_metabolism","proposed_value":"Not metabolized; renal excretion","rationale":"FDA labeling states gabapentin is eliminated by renal excretion as unchanged drug and is not appreciably metabolized in humans.","requires_user_approval":false,"status":"verified","supporting_sources":["https://www.accessdata.fda.gov/drugsatfda_docs/label/2024/021129s056%2C020882s057%2C020235s076lbl.pdf"]},{"current_value":"Renal/no major metabolism","field":"filter_metabolism","proposed_value":"Renal/no major metabolism","rationale":"Supported by FDA labeling and NCBI sources.","requires_user_approval":false,"status":"verified","supporting_sources":["https://www.accessdata.fda.gov/drugsatfda_docs/label/2024/021129s056%2C020882s057%2C020235s076lbl.pdf","https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK493228/"]},{"current_value":"5-7 h","field":"half_life_range","proposed_value":"5-7 h","rationale":"FDA labeling and NCBI sources state gabapentin elimination half-life is 5 to 7 hours in normal renal function.","requires_user_approval":false,"status":"verified","supporting_sources":["https://www.accessdata.fda.gov/drugsatfda_docs/label/2024/021129s056%2C020882s057%2C020235s076lbl.pdf","https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK2511/table/A252/"]},{"current_value":"Precise antiepileptic mechanism is unknown; gabapentin binds the alpha-2-delta subunit of voltage-activated calcium channels, but the relationship of this binding to efficacy is not fully established.","field":"mechanism_of_action","proposed_value":"Precise antiepileptic mechanism is unknown; gabapentin binds the alpha-2-delta subunit of voltage-activated calcium channels, but the relationship of this binding to efficacy is not fully established.","rationale":"FDA labeling supports each part of the mechanism statement.","requires_user_approval":false,"status":"verified","supporting_sources":["https://www.accessdata.fda.gov/drugsatfda_docs/label/2024/021129s056%2C020882s057%2C020235s076lbl.pdf"]},{"current_value":"FDA/DailyMed labeling","field":"mechanism_source","proposed_value":"FDA-approved Neurontin prescribing information (accessdata.fda.gov)","rationale":"The mechanism statement is supported by FDA labeling; DailyMed should not be named as the source under the trusted-domain policy.","requires_user_approval":false,"status":"incorrect","supporting_sources":["https://www.accessdata.fda.gov/drugsatfda_docs/label/2024/021129s056%2C020882s057%2C020235s076lbl.pdf"]},{"current_value":"FDA label","field":"mechanism_source_tier","proposed_value":"FDA label","rationale":"The cited accessdata.fda.gov label is FDA labeling.","requires_user_approval":false,"status":"verified","supporting_sources":["https://www.accessdata.fda.gov/drugsatfda_docs/label/2024/021129s056%2C020882s057%2C020235s076lbl.pdf"]},{"current_value":"Moderate","field":"mechanism_confidence","proposed_value":"Moderate","rationale":"The label states the precise mechanism and the relationship of alpha-2-delta binding to therapeutic effects are unknown, supporting a non-high confidence rating.","requires_user_approval":false,"status":"verified","supporting_sources":["https://www.accessdata.fda.gov/drugsatfda_docs/label/2024/021129s056%2C020882s057%2C020235s076lbl.pdf"]},{"current_value":"900 mg/day","field":"minimum_effective_dose","proposed_value":"900 mg/day","rationale":"FDA labeling gives a starting dose of 300 mg three times daily and a recommended maintenance range of 300 to 600 mg three times daily; clinical study text reports a statistically significant responder-rate difference at 900 mg/day.","requires_user_approval":false,"status":"verified","supporting_sources":["https://www.accessdata.fda.gov/drugsatfda_docs/label/2024/021129s056%2C020882s057%2C020235s076lbl.pdf"]},{"current_value":"1800 mg/day labeled for epilepsy; higher doses used in practice","field":"maximum_approved_daily_dose","proposed_value":"1800 mg/day recommended maintenance dose for epilepsy; FDA labeling notes 2400 mg/day in long-term studies and 3600 mg/day short-term in a small number of patients","rationale":"The current value is directionally supported, but the proposed wording is closer to the FDA label and avoids implying that all higher dosing is merely extra-label practice.","requires_user_approval":false,"status":"verified","supporting_sources":["https://www.accessdata.fda.gov/drugsatfda_docs/label/2024/021129s056%2C020882s057%2C020235s076lbl.pdf"]},{"current_value":"Adjunctive focal seizures: 900-1800 mg/day divided TID; up to 3600 mg/day used","field":"typical_doses_per_day","proposed_value":"Adjunctive focal/partial-onset seizures: 900-1800 mg/day divided TID; FDA labeling notes doses up to 3600 mg/day have been administered short-term in a small number of patients","rationale":"FDA labeling supports 300 to 600 mg three times daily for patients 12 years and older and notes higher administered doses.","requires_user_approval":false,"status":"verified","supporting_sources":["https://www.accessdata.fda.gov/drugsatfda_docs/label/2024/021129s056%2C020882s057%2C020235s076lbl.pdf"]},{"current_value":"CNS: somnolence 19%, CNS: dizziness 17%, neurologic: ataxia 13%, constitutional: fatigue 11%, neurologic: nystagmus 8%, infectious: viral infection 11%","field":"adverse_symptoms_percentages","proposed_value":"CNS: somnolence 19%, CNS: dizziness 17%, neurologic: ataxia 13%, constitutional: fatigue 11%, neurologic: nystagmus 8%, infectious: viral infection 11%","rationale":"The adult epilepsy placebo-controlled add-on trial table supports somnolence, dizziness, ataxia, fatigue, and nystagmus percentages; the pediatric epilepsy table supports viral infection 11%.","requires_user_approval":false,"status":"verified","supporting_sources":["https://www.accessdata.fda.gov/drugsatfda_docs/label/2024/021129s056%2C020882s057%2C020235s076lbl.pdf"]},{"current_value":"No clinically meaningful QT effect established","field":"qt_interval_effect","proposed_value":"No QT warning or clinically meaningful QT effect described in FDA gabapentin labeling","rationale":"The current wording is plausible, but no direct QT-specific trusted source was identified in the cited row sources. FDA gabapentin labeling does not provide a direct QT claim.","requires_user_approval":false,"status":"missing_source","supporting_sources":["https://www.accessdata.fda.gov/drugsatfda_docs/label/2024/021129s056%2C020882s057%2C020235s076lbl.pdf"]},{"current_value":"No known meaningful QT effect","field":"filter_qt_effect","proposed_value":"No QT warning identified in FDA labeling","rationale":"No direct QT-specific source was cited; this should be worded as a label-based absence of warning unless a direct QT study/source is added.","requires_user_approval":false,"status":"missing_source","supporting_sources":["https://www.accessdata.fda.gov/drugsatfda_docs/label/2024/021129s056%2C020882s057%2C020235s076lbl.pdf"]},{"current_value":"1993","field":"year_fda_cleared","proposed_value":"1993","rationale":"FDA labeling states Initial U.S. Approval: 1993; an FDA safety communication also states gabapentin was first approved in 1993.","requires_user_approval":false,"status":"verified","supporting_sources":["https://www.accessdata.fda.gov/drugsatfda_docs/label/2024/021129s056%2C020882s057%2C020235s076lbl.pdf","https://www.fda.gov/safety/medical-product-safety-information/neurontin-gralise-horizant-gabapentin-and-lyrica-lyrica-cr-pregabalin-drug-safety-communication"]},{"current_value":"NCBI LiverTox anticonvulsants table; Epilepsy Society ASM list; Epilepsy Foundation Australia ASM list; FDA/DailyMed labeling","field":"evidence_sources","proposed_value":"FDA-approved Neurontin labeling (accessdata.fda.gov); FDA Gralise and Horizant labels for trade-name disambiguation; NCBI Bookshelf LiverTox/StatPearls; PubMed placebo-controlled gabapentin RCT records; FDA drug safety communication","rationale":"Current evidence_sources includes DailyMed and non-whitelisted organizations. The proposed source list uses trusted domains and better maps to the row facts.","requires_user_approval":false,"status":"incorrect","supporting_sources":["https://www.accessdata.fda.gov/drugsatfda_docs/label/2024/021129s056%2C020882s057%2C020235s076lbl.pdf","https://www.accessdata.fda.gov/drugsatfda_docs/label/2023/022544s029lbl.pdf","https://www.accessdata.fda.gov/drugsatfda_docs/label/2020/022399s010lbl.pdf","https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK548252/","https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK493228/","https://www.fda.gov/safety/medical-product-safety-information/neurontin-gralise-horizant-gabapentin-and-lyrica-lyrica-cr-pregabalin-drug-safety-communication"]}],"generic_name":"gabapentin","missing_rcts":[],"outcome_checks":[{"current_value":"15.2-17.9 % (drug minus placebo RR50 differential at maximum effective dose/regimen: Anhut1994 gabapentin 1200 mg/day 17.9%; PMID1990 gabapentin 1200 mg/day 15.2%)","field":"diff_50_responder_maximum_effective_dose","proposed_value":"15.2-17.9 % (drug minus placebo RR50 differential at maximum effective dose/regimen: Anhut1994 gabapentin 1200 mg/day 17.9%; PMID1990 gabapentin 1200 mg/day 15.2%)","rationale":"PMID 1971862 reports 25.0% responders with gabapentin 1200 mg/day vs 9.8% placebo, differential 15.2 points. Anhut1994 is supported by the cited RCT/outcome extraction as 28.0% vs 10.1%, differential 17.9 points.","requires_user_approval":false,"status":"verified","supporting_sources":["https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/1971862/","https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/8082624/"]},{"current_value":"Anhut1994|17.9|https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/8082624/|272; PMID1990|15.2|https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/1971862/|127","field":"plot_diff_50_responder_maximum_effective_dose","proposed_value":"Anhut1994|17.9|https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/8082624/|272; PMID1990|15.2|https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/1971862/|127","rationale":"The plotted values match the cited RR50 differentials and study totals in the outcome bundle.","requires_user_approval":false,"status":"verified","supporting_sources":["https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/8082624/","https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/1971862/"]},{"current_value":"10.5-16.7 % (drug minus placebo MPC differential at maximum effective dose/regimen: Appleton1999 gabapentin 23-35 mg/kg/day 10.5%; PMID1990 gabapentin 1200 mg/day 16.7%)","field":"diff_median_pct_change_maximum_effective_dose","proposed_value":"10.5-16.7 % (drug minus placebo MPC differential at maximum effective dose/regimen: Appleton1999 gabapentin 23-35 mg/kg/day 10.5%; PMID1990 gabapentin 1200 mg/day 16.7%)","rationale":"Appleton1999 reports median percentage change -17.0% with gabapentin and -6.5% with placebo, a 10.5-point differential. PMID1990 reports median partial-seizure reduction 29.2% vs 12.5%, a 16.7-point differential.","requires_user_approval":false,"status":"verified","supporting_sources":["https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/10448830/","https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/1971862/"]},{"current_value":"Appleton1999|10.5|https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/10448830/|247; PMID1990|16.7|https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/1971862/|127","field":"plot_diff_median_pct_change_maximum_effective_dose","proposed_value":"Appleton1999|10.5|https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/10448830/|247; PMID1990|16.7|https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/1971862/|127","rationale":"The plotted values match the cited MPC calculations.","requires_user_approval":false,"status":"verified","supporting_sources":["https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/10448830/","https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/1971862/"]},{"current_value":"NR/not extractable as a drug-minus-placebo seizure-freedom patient-rate differential at the maximum effective dose/regimen from included phase II/III placebo-controlled RCT records","field":"diff_seizure_freedom_maximum_effective_dose","proposed_value":"1.7 % (drug minus placebo seizure-freedom patient-rate differential: Appleton1999 gabapentin 23-35 mg/kg/day, 3/119 [2.5%] vs placebo 1/128 [0.8%])","rationale":"Appleton1999 reports three gabapentin patients and one placebo patient were seizure-free during double-blind treatment. FDA Neurontin pediatric trial tables provide the gabapentin/placebo denominators of 119 and 128, allowing an approximate patient-rate differential of 1.7 percentage points.","requires_user_approval":true,"status":"incorrect","supporting_sources":["https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/10448830/","https://www.accessdata.fda.gov/drugsatfda_docs/label/2024/021129s056%2C020882s057%2C020235s076lbl.pdf"]},{"current_value":"","field":"plot_diff_seizure_freedom_maximum_effective_dose","proposed_value":"Appleton1999|1.7|https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/10448830/|247","rationale":"This plot entry follows from the Appleton1999 seizure-free counts and FDA label denominators. User approval is appropriate because it changes an NR field to an extracted value.","requires_user_approval":true,"status":"incorrect","supporting_sources":["https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/10448830/","https://www.accessdata.fda.gov/drugsatfda_docs/label/2024/021129s056%2C020882s057%2C020235s076lbl.pdf"]}],"overall_status":"needs_update","proposed_row_updates":[{"current_value":"Gralise; Horizant; Neurontin","field":"trade_names","proposed_value":"Gralise; Neurontin","reason":"Horizant is gabapentin enacarbil, not gabapentin, and FDA labeling states it is not the same medicine as Neurontin/Gralise and should not be used in their place.","requires_user_approval":true,"source_urls":["https://www.accessdata.fda.gov/drugsatfda_docs/label/2020/022399s010lbl.pdf","https://www.accessdata.fda.gov/drugsatfda_docs/label/2024/021129s056%2C020882s057%2C020235s076lbl.pdf","https://www.accessdata.fda.gov/drugsatfda_docs/label/2023/022544s029lbl.pdf"]},{"current_value":"","field":"pubmed_search_aliases","proposed_value":"GBP","reason":"GBP is used as an abbreviation for gabapentin in PubMed RCT abstracts and should be captured as a search alias.","requires_user_approval":false,"source_urls":["https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/10448830/","https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/1907907/"]},{"current_value":"No FDA boxed warning identified in selected current DailyMed label.","field":"fda_black_box_warning","proposed_value":"No FDA boxed warning identified in current FDA-approved Neurontin (gabapentin) prescribing information.","reason":"FDA-source-only policy for boxed warnings; current text cites DailyMed and a Horizant label.","requires_user_approval":false,"source_urls":["https://www.accessdata.fda.gov/drugsatfda_docs/label/2024/021129s056%2C020882s057%2C020235s076lbl.pdf"]},{"current_value":"FDA/DailyMed SPL; status=no_boxed_warning_in_selected_label; setid=4c486fc7-c8c4-4c6c-b30c-366dabaeaadd; published=May 07, 2025; title=HORIZANT (GABAPENTIN ENACARBIL) TABLET, EXTENDED RELEASE [AZURITY PHARMACEUTICALS, INC. (FORMERLY ARBOR PHARMACEUTICALS)]; url=https://dailymed.nlm.nih.gov/dailymed/drugInfo.cfm?setid=4c486fc7-c8c4-4c6c-b30c-366dabaeaadd","field":"fda_black_box_warning_source","proposed_value":"FDA-approved Neurontin prescribing information; status=no_boxed_warning_identified_in_fda_label; revised=07/2024; title=NEURONTIN (gabapentin) capsules/tablets/oral solution; url=https://www.accessdata.fda.gov/drugsatfda_docs/label/2024/021129s056%2C020882s057%2C020235s076lbl.pdf","reason":"Replaces nonpermissible DailyMed/Horizant source with FDA accessdata gabapentin labeling.","requires_user_approval":false,"source_urls":["https://www.accessdata.fda.gov/drugsatfda_docs/label/2024/021129s056%2C020882s057%2C020235s076lbl.pdf"]},{"current_value":"05-20-2026","field":"fda_black_box_warning_verified","proposed_value":"05-21-2026","reason":"Audit verification was performed against FDA labeling on the current audit date.","requires_user_approval":false,"source_urls":["https://www.accessdata.fda.gov/drugsatfda_docs/label/2024/021129s056%2C020882s057%2C020235s076lbl.pdf"]},{"current_value":"Inducer; No major enzyme effect","field":"filter_enzyme_effect","proposed_value":"No major enzyme effect","reason":"Gabapentin is not a hepatic enzyme inducer; current filter contains a direct contradiction.","requires_user_approval":true,"source_urls":["https://www.accessdata.fda.gov/drugsatfda_docs/label/2024/021129s056%2C020882s057%2C020235s076lbl.pdf","https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/7693432/"]},{"current_value":"NR/not extractable as a drug-minus-placebo seizure-freedom patient-rate differential at the maximum effective dose/regimen from included phase II/III placebo-controlled RCT records","field":"diff_seizure_freedom_maximum_effective_dose","proposed_value":"1.7 % (drug minus placebo seizure-freedom patient-rate differential: Appleton1999 gabapentin 23-35 mg/kg/day, 3/119 [2.5%] vs placebo 1/128 [0.8%])","reason":"Appleton1999 reports seizure-free counts, and FDA label denominators allow rate calculation.","requires_user_approval":true,"source_urls":["https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/10448830/","https://www.accessdata.fda.gov/drugsatfda_docs/label/2024/021129s056%2C020882s057%2C020235s076lbl.pdf"]},{"current_value":"","field":"plot_diff_seizure_freedom_maximum_effective_dose","proposed_value":"Appleton1999|1.7|https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/10448830/|247","reason":"Adds the extractable Appleton1999 seizure-freedom differential.","requires_user_approval":true,"source_urls":["https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/10448830/","https://www.accessdata.fda.gov/drugsatfda_docs/label/2024/021129s056%2C020882s057%2C020235s076lbl.pdf"]},{"current_value":"FDA/DailyMed labeling","field":"mechanism_source","proposed_value":"FDA-approved Neurontin prescribing information (accessdata.fda.gov)","reason":"The mechanism is supported by FDA labeling; the row should cite the FDA label rather than DailyMed.","requires_user_approval":false,"source_urls":["https://www.accessdata.fda.gov/drugsatfda_docs/label/2024/021129s056%2C020882s057%2C020235s076lbl.pdf"]},{"current_value":"NCBI LiverTox anticonvulsants table; Epilepsy Society ASM list; Epilepsy Foundation Australia ASM list; FDA/DailyMed labeling","field":"evidence_sources","proposed_value":"FDA-approved Neurontin labeling (accessdata.fda.gov); FDA Gralise and Horizant labels for trade-name disambiguation; NCBI Bookshelf LiverTox/StatPearls; PubMed placebo-controlled gabapentin RCT records; FDA drug safety communication","reason":"Uses trusted-domain sources that actually support the populated cells and removes DailyMed/non-whitelisted source names.","requires_user_approval":false,"source_urls":["https://www.accessdata.fda.gov/drugsatfda_docs/label/2024/021129s056%2C020882s057%2C020235s076lbl.pdf","https://www.accessdata.fda.gov/drugsatfda_docs/label/2023/022544s029lbl.pdf","https://www.accessdata.fda.gov/drugsatfda_docs/label/2020/022399s010lbl.pdf","https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK548252/","https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK493228/","https://www.fda.gov/safety/medical-product-safety-information/neurontin-gralise-horizant-gabapentin-and-lyrica-lyrica-cr-pregabalin-drug-safety-communication"]},{"current_value":"Current ASM for focal/partial seizures; also used for postherpetic neuralgia, neuropathic pain, restless legs syndrome, and other off-label uses.","field":"status_or_notes","proposed_value":"Current ASM for adjunctive treatment of focal/partial-onset seizures; also indicated for postherpetic neuralgia. Gabapentin enacarbil (Horizant) is a distinct prodrug indicated for RLS/PHN and should not be treated as an interchangeable gabapentin trade name.","reason":"Clarifies the Horizant/gabapentin-enacarbil distinction while preserving the clinically relevant notes.","requires_user_approval":true,"source_urls":["https://www.accessdata.fda.gov/drugsatfda_docs/label/2024/021129s056%2C020882s057%2C020235s076lbl.pdf","https://www.accessdata.fda.gov/drugsatfda_docs/label/2020/022399s010lbl.pdf"]}],"rct_link_checks":[{"label":"Yamauchi2006","pmid":"16884455","proposed_action":"retain","rationale":"PubMed record supports gabapentin, randomized assignment, double-blind placebo-controlled parallel-group design, refractory partial seizures, and 1200/1800 mg/day adjunctive therapy.","status":"verified","url":"https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/16884455/"},{"label":"Zhu2005","pmid":"15774213","proposed_action":"retain","rationale":"PubMed abstract supports gabapentin vs placebo in a double-blind multicenter refractory epilepsy trial. Outcome percentages for RR50/MPC/SF are not extractable from the abstract.","status":"verified","url":"https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/15774213/"},{"label":"Appleton1999","pmid":"10448830","proposed_action":"retain","rationale":"PubMed abstract supports a 12-week multicentre, double-blind, placebo-controlled randomized pediatric refractory partial-seizure trial of gabapentin add-on therapy.","status":"verified","url":"https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/10448830/"},{"label":"Leach1997","pmid":"9120451","proposed_action":"retain with outcome caveat","rationale":"Record supports a double-blind, dose-ranging, placebo-controlled crossover study of adjunctive gabapentin in refractory epilepsy, but seizure-frequency RR50/MPC/SF outcomes are not extractable from the abstract.","status":"verified","url":"https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/9120451/"},{"label":"Trudeau1996","pmid":"9120226","proposed_action":"retain with indication caveat","rationale":"The publication reports two double-blind, placebo-controlled multicenter gabapentin studies in childhood absence epilepsy. It verifies drug/design/placebo but does not support focal-seizure efficacy.","status":"verified","url":"https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/9120226/"},{"label":"Dimond1996","pmid":"8771597","proposed_action":"flag for user review before retaining as a phase II/III RCT link","rationale":"The abstract describes a review/secondary analysis of global improvement data from five double-blind clinical trials, not an independent primary phase II/III seizure-efficacy RCT report. It should not be used for RR50/MPC/SF outcomes.","status":"not_phase_ii_iii","url":"https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/8771597/"},{"label":"BenMenachem1995","pmid":"8536677","proposed_action":"retain with outcome caveat","rationale":"PubMed supports gabapentin in a placebo-controlled double-blind study in intractable complex partial seizures, with RCT publication type. The abstract focuses on CSF parameters and does not provide extractable RR50/MPC/SF outcomes.","status":"verified","url":"https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/8536677/"},{"label":"Anhut1994","pmid":"8082624","proposed_action":"retain","rationale":"Publication is a multicenter, double-blind, randomized, placebo-controlled add-on gabapentin study in refractory partial seizures and supports the RR50 differential used in the row.","status":"verified","url":"https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/8082624/"},{"label":"PMID1993","pmid":"8232945","proposed_action":"retain; consider relabeling as USGabapentinStudyGroup1993","rationale":"PubMed/Neurology abstract supports a 12-week multicenter, placebo-controlled, parallel-group gabapentin add-on trial in refractory partial epilepsy. The abstract provides responder-rate range but not a maximum-dose-specific differential without supplemental sources.","status":"verified","url":"https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/8232945/"},{"label":"Sivenius1991","pmid":"1907907","proposed_action":"retain with outcome caveat","rationale":"PubMed supports a double-blind, placebo-controlled add-on gabapentin study in partial and secondarily generalized seizures. The abstract reports a 57% decrease for the 1200 mg arm but not enough placebo-arm percentage detail for the row's plotted differential outcomes.","status":"verified","url":"https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/1907907/"},{"label":"PMID1990","pmid":"1971862","proposed_action":"retain; consider relabeling as UKGabapentinStudyGroup1990","rationale":"The deterministic warning is not upheld. The abstract explicitly states multicentre, double-blind, placebo-controlled, parallel-group gabapentin add-on therapy in drug-resistant partial epilepsy, and reports the RR50 and MPC values used in the row.","status":"verified","url":"https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/1971862/"}],"source_updates":[{"current_value":"FDA/DailyMed SPL; status=no_boxed_warning_in_selected_label; setid=4c486fc7-c8c4-4c6c-b30c-366dabaeaadd; published=May 07, 2025; title=HORIZANT (GABAPENTIN ENACARBIL) TABLET, EXTENDED RELEASE [AZURITY PHARMACEUTICALS, INC. (FORMERLY ARBOR PHARMACEUTICALS)]; url=https://dailymed.nlm.nih.gov/dailymed/drugInfo.cfm?setid=4c486fc7-c8c4-4c6c-b30c-366dabaeaadd","field":"fda_black_box_warning_source","proposed_value":"FDA-approved Neurontin prescribing information; status=no_boxed_warning_identified_in_fda_label; revised=07/2024; title=NEURONTIN (gabapentin) capsules/tablets/oral solution; url=https://www.accessdata.fda.gov/drugsatfda_docs/label/2024/021129s056%2C020882s057%2C020235s076lbl.pdf","reason":"FDA-only boxed warning policy and correct gabapentin product source.","requires_user_approval":false,"source_urls":["https://www.accessdata.fda.gov/drugsatfda_docs/label/2024/021129s056%2C020882s057%2C020235s076lbl.pdf"]},{"current_value":"FDA/DailyMed labeling","field":"mechanism_source","proposed_value":"FDA-approved Neurontin prescribing information (accessdata.fda.gov)","reason":"Mechanism text is supported by FDA label; DailyMed is not a trusted-domain citation in this audit.","requires_user_approval":false,"source_urls":["https://www.accessdata.fda.gov/drugsatfda_docs/label/2024/021129s056%2C020882s057%2C020235s076lbl.pdf"]},{"current_value":"NCBI LiverTox anticonvulsants table; Epilepsy Society ASM list; Epilepsy Foundation Australia ASM list; FDA/DailyMed labeling","field":"evidence_sources","proposed_value":"FDA-approved Neurontin labeling (accessdata.fda.gov); FDA Gralise and Horizant labels for trade-name disambiguation; NCBI Bookshelf LiverTox/StatPearls; PubMed placebo-controlled gabapentin RCT records; FDA drug safety communication","reason":"Replaces non-trusted or nonspecific source names with sources that support the populated cells.","requires_user_approval":false,"source_urls":["https://www.accessdata.fda.gov/drugsatfda_docs/label/2024/021129s056%2C020882s057%2C020235s076lbl.pdf","https://www.accessdata.fda.gov/drugsatfda_docs/label/2023/022544s029lbl.pdf","https://www.accessdata.fda.gov/drugsatfda_docs/label/2020/022399s010lbl.pdf","https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK548252/","https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK493228/","https://www.fda.gov/safety/medical-product-safety-information/neurontin-gralise-horizant-gabapentin-and-lyrica-lyrica-cr-pregabalin-drug-safety-communication"]}],"summary":"Core gabapentin pharmacology, dosing, formulation, adverse-event, focal-seizure indication, year-approval, and most RCT-link facts are supported by FDA/NCBI/PubMed sources. Updates are needed for the boxed-warning source, Horizant trade-name handling, enzyme-effect filter, trusted evidence source names, and seizure-freedom extraction from Appleton1999. PMID 1971862 is a valid gabapentin placebo-controlled RCT despite its nonspecific title."}
